            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Before the new one: say the Mandarin for to know someone, then the Mandarin for friend."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 1,
          "type": "input",
          "title": "You'll want to know: gōngzuò",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "gōngzuò — \"to work; job\"."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "wǒ jiějie gōngzuò, wǒ shàngxué — My sister works; I go to school."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Its characters come later; for now it is a word for the ear."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "etymology",
          "title": "The word, taken apart",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "gōngzuò is built from gōng 'craft' and zuò 'to do', both inherited from Middle Chinese, kuwng and tsak (Guangyun, in Baxter's transcription); the dictionary glosses the first 'office' and 'skill', the second 'to do'."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 3,
          "type": "notice",
          "title": "What you've built",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "One more word about people."
